  </p>

  <h2>12. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    Ebmeyer Consulting is liable for intent, including unlawful intent, and gross
    negligence. Liability for slight negligence is excluded to the extent permitted by law.
    Mandatory statutory liability, in particular for personal injury and mandatory data
    protection liability, remains reserved.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the case of slight negligence, liability for indirect damage, consequential damage,
    lost profit and business interruption is excluded to the extent permitted by law. For
    direct damage caused by slight negligence, liability is limited to the net fee owed for
    the specific service that directly caused the damage, but in no event exceeding the net
    fee agreed for that service. In multi-phase programmes, each individual phase
    constitutes a specific service. This exclusion and limitation of liability do not apply
    in cases of intent, gross negligence, personal injury, mandatory data protection
    liability or other mandatory statutory liability. Art. 100 and Art. 101 CO and, where
    applicable, Art. 8 of the Swiss Federal Act against Unfair Competition (UCA) remain
    reserved.
  </p>
  <p>
    In addition to the above liability provisions, and unless mandatory law provides
    otherwise, Ebmeyer Consulting is not liable for the Client's entrepreneurial decisions,
    implementation by third parties, or the availability of or changes to third-party
    tools. Ebmeyer Consulting's liability for its own culpable breaches of duty remains
    unaffected. Section 9 applies to the professional review of AI-generated results.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Termination</h2>
  <p>
    The contract term follows the quotation or order confirmation. For retainers expressly
    agreed in the future, the term is 6 or 12 months. They may be terminated with one
    month's notice to the end of the term, insofar as this is compatible with the right to
    terminate at any time under Art. 404 CO, and are not renewed automatically without
    express consent. The right of both parties to terminate at any time under Art. 404 CO
    remains reserved.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the contract ends by notice, dissolution or for any other reason before all services
    have been fully rendered, the Client owes the fee for the services actually rendered up
    to that point, as well as reimbursement of necessary expenses and non-cancellable
    commitments entered into, insofar as these have not already been otherwise remunerated
    or were avoidable.
  </p>
  <p>
    For mandates, the right to terminate at any time under Art. 404 CO remains reserved. In
    the event of termination under Art. 404 CO, no fee is owed for services not yet
    rendered. In the event of termination at an inopportune time, statutory claims for
    damages under Art. 404 para. 2 CO remain reserved. In multi-phase programmes, fully
    completed phases are owed in full. For phases begun but not yet completed, the fee is
    owed pro rata according to the demonstrably determined state of performance. Phases not
    yet begun are not owed, subject to statutory claims. Insofar as deliverables expressly
    designated as capable of acceptance are to be qualified as contracts for work, the
    statutory consequences of termination and remuneration, in particular Art. 377 CO,
    remain reserved. The consequences of force majeure are governed exclusively by
    Section 14.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either party may terminate the contract with immediate effect for good cause, in
    particular in the event of a serious breach of contract or payment default of more than
    30 days despite a reminder.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Force Majeure</h2>
  <p>
    Events beyond a party's control – such as natural events, official orders, strikes,
    cyberattacks or the failure of telecommunications, cloud or AI services – release the
    affected party from its performance obligations for the duration and to the extent of
    the impediment. In the case of a temporary impediment, the services are postponed within
    a reasonable period or, where possible and reasonable, rendered by way of an equivalent
    substitute service.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the event results in the definitive impossibility of performance or lasts longer than
    60 calendar days, either party may terminate the contract with respect to the services
    not yet rendered. In that case, only services already rendered and necessary,
    non-cancellable costs incurred in connection with the mandate are owed, insofar as these
    have not already been otherwise remunerated. No remuneration is owed for services not
    rendered. The statutory consequences regarding restitution and impossibility, in
    particular under Art. 119 CO, as well as liability for breaches of duty for which a
    party is responsible, remain reserved.
  </p>

